Excelsior! Poker Solitaire®                       Copyright 1993 Sean Workman
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Quickly typed Sysop docs - please excuse any tpyos <-- example!  *B)

Setting up Excelsior!  Poker Solitaire is pretty easy.  Just make a
directory called 'Poker' (or whatever you want to call it) in your 'Doors:'
directory, edit line 30 to reflect the game's directory (if it's not
Doors:Poker).  The game will create a new Top-10 file if one's not present.
If you ever want to reset it, just delete it.  Since there's a limit to the
high scores available, you might want to set up your cron program to delete
the scores once a month...

This game auto-selects output type.  Preferred it IBM/ANSI.  With that
terminal type, you get the darker green background (which I prefer) and the
double-lined box surrounding the score table on the right.  If you want to
see how the other terminal types see the game, just use the TS command and
play a game.  This game will not function under the ASCII terminal type, it
politely explains to the user the reason the game won't function and then
exits.

This game is (obviously) written in Arexx.  I've got a 25mhz 68030 card and
it runs VERY nicely (a 14.4k caller couldn't move faster than the game
could keep up!) although I'm not sure a standard 7.14mhz Amiga would be as
peppy.  I don't know anyone running Excelsior!  that doesn't have an
accelerator (that's a strange thought that just occured to me...) I've
optimized the output as much as I can for low baud rate callers (it only
outputs the cards being changed after a swap to minimize unnecessary text
output.

For the game:  This game can be found in almost any Book of Hoyle.  It's a
lesser know game, but quite fun anyway.  It's very strategic.  If you
always get scores less than 250 points, you've either missed a key idea in
the gameplay or have been dealt bad cards a lot (it's possible...  they ARE
random...) Remember, improving one hand almost ALWAYS screws up another...
plan carefully and don't S)core too quickly...

If you like this game, please encourage me to write another one by A)
sending me the shareware fee -- it's not much, but it WOULD be nice to see
at least ONE person register it...  or B) at least leave me an honest
opinion on the Excelsior! support BBS (my handle is The Trooper).

***** V1.3  (1/14/93)
Fixed tight looping bug that occurred when carrier was lost or user was
dumped.  Now logs these actions into the main log.  Anybody know how to fix
the chat bug?  Break in for a chat and then exit to see what I mean...
Minor code optimization.

***** V1.2  (1/12/93)
Added hand by hand scoring next to the horizontal hands, and under the
vertical hand being scored.

***** V1.1  (1/11/93)
Since Excelsior! has trouble displaying the IBM font's characters for the
card suits, I changed the program to use those special characters if the
person was set to IBM/ANSI and their baud rate was not 19200 (local baud
rate).  If someone knows of a better way to do this, let me know.  This
involved changing the FLUSH scoring routines from absolute to relative
scoring (instead of them all being hearts, clubs, etc they are now scored
if they are all the same, no matter what they are.)

Fixed another lame JrComm output bug.  JrComm has a couple flaky ANSI code
interpretations and I always seem to hit on them.  In EPSv1.0 you would
sometimes get a black square when you moved the cursor off the current
card.  This should be fixed (I can't get it to happen anymore...)

***** V1.0  (1/10/93)
First public release of EPS.  All things SEEM to function perfectly on my
end (and via testing with select users).  Maybe the rest of the world can
tell me if something doesn't work right...
Top-10 list added

***** V0.9  (1/9/93)
Fully functional game.  All seems to work as planned.  The only unsupported
feature that _I_ want is a Top-10 list.

***** V0.0  (1/1/93)
I had a New Year's resolution.  I wanted to write a Quality game
specifically (and only) for the greatest BBS system available, Excelsior!
I had to think of an idea for a game... then I remembered a game I wrote
a long time ago on my Apple //e and decided to write it again since I'd
never seen a Poker Solitaire game online anywhere before (originality is
my thing!)

